---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/deliverability";
const title = "Shopify email deliverability: diagnose before sending more";
const desc = "For the operator investigating rejected messages, complaints or a sudden delivery change. Start with the affected recipient domain and message stream; a store-wide open-rate average cannot identify where a failure occurs.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does successful delivery mean inbox placement?",
    "answer": "No. A receiving server accepting a message does not establish where it was displayed."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every store use the same sunset period?",
    "answer": "Choose and document a policy for the audience\u2019s buying cycle and available engagement evidence; do not treat an open-only cutoff as universal."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can switching platforms solve an incident?",
    "answer": "First identify the failing stream and cause. A switch also requires authentication, suppression and queue reconciliation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Where are the current Gmail requirements?",
    "answer": "Use Google\u2019s linked sender guidelines and their bulk-sender section. This guide is an operational starting point, not a complete provider checklist."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl">{desc}</p>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Separate the symptoms</h2><p>Record the sending platform, From domain, recipient domain, campaign or flow, timestamps and response codes. Distinguish a rejected message from an accepted message reported in spam. Acceptance is not proof of inbox placement. Compare the affected stream with an unchanged stream before changing templates or moving providers.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Check authentication on delivered messages</h2><p>Inspect headers from a controlled send through the actual production sending path. Google requires SPF or DKIM for all senders to personal Gmail accounts; its bulk-sender requirements add SPF, DKIM and DMARC, alignment, and one-click unsubscribe for marketing and subscribed messages. The linked Google guidance contains the full requirements and scope. Do not infer authentication success from a green DNS setup screen alone.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Map every sender before changing DNS</h2><p>List the systems sending campaigns, order updates, support replies and other mail. Assign a DNS owner and preserve a rollback record. Ask the sending provider to explain its required records and test each stream after a change. A separate subdomain is an organizational boundary, not a promise that order messages are insulated from reputation problems. Do not advance a DMARC enforcement policy on an arbitrary calendar without checking legitimate senders.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Investigate the audience and queue</h2><p>Trace complaints or bounces to signup source, import batch, campaign and recipient domain. Review how unsubscribe and suppression events reach queued automations. Google recommends sending to people who want the messages and making unsubscribe easy. Pause the affected promotional enrollment while its source is investigated; do not use another provider to bypass a suppression. Define inactivity using the store’s actual purchase and engagement cycle rather than a universal no-open deadline.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Plan a controlled resumption</h2><p>Proposed incident drill: an imported audience starts receiving a launch campaign and one recipient domain begins rejecting it. Preserve the response codes, stop the affected queue, check authentication and source records, and ask the provider to interpret the errors. Resume only against documented acceptance conditions. Record the observed outcome; this is a proposed drill, not a reported recovery or a fixed warmup schedule.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Choose a tool by its incident evidence</h2><p>Ask Sequenzy, Klaviyo or another candidate to demonstrate per-domain response logs, suppression propagation, queued-message cancellation and access to authentication results. Ask who handles escalation when logs are incomplete. These are selection requirements, not verified feature claims. Retain order-update ownership during any migration and reconcile suppression records before changing send paths.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Keep an incident record</h2><p>Log what changed, who approved it, affected streams and the next review point. Track rejected messages and complaints with their denominators and time windows; compare like-for-like cohorts. A seed test is one observation, not proof of every subscriber’s placement. Authentication, relevant content and provider requirements do not guarantee inbox delivery.</p></section>
    <p>Provider reference: <a class="link" href="https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126?hl=en">Google email sender guidelines</a> (reviewed November 25, 2026).</p>
    <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount strategy</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">holiday gifting</a> or the <a class="link" href="/use-cases">workflow hub</a>.</p>
    <p><a class="link" href="/apps">Explore app candidates</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare">Compare requirements</a> · <a class="link" href="/for">Store-type guides</a></p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions about this workflow" />
</BaseLayout>
